<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         name="CleanCode"
         xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="../vendor/squizlabs/php_codesniffer/phpcs.xsd">
    <description>Custom sniffs implementing the clean-code standards defined at mikebronner.dev/clean-code. Every sniff in this standard's Sniffs/ directory is included automatically when the standard is referenced.</description>

    <!--
        CleanCode.Metrics.NumberOfChildren.OrdinalIndex is instrumentation, not
        a standard: it reports what the ordinal index cost a token stream, for
        the benefit of one test that can only read the sniff's counters out of
        a real run's own report. Nothing a consumer installs this package for
        asks for it.

        The sniff gates it on the cleancode_ordinal_index_diagnostic config
        value, and a config value is not a safe gate on its own. PHPCS merges
        three routes into one store, and the sniff cannot tell them apart: the
        runtime-set flag, a ruleset `config` element, and the config-set
        command, which *persists* into the install's shared CodeSniffer.conf. A
        stale config-set on a shared install (a CI runner, a monorepo, a cached
        Docker layer) therefore leaks this diagnostic into every later ordinary
        run against that install, on every file holding a class declaration,
        until someone runs config-delete. That is a warning a downstream
        consumer cannot diagnose from their own repository.

        Severity 0 closes the hazard at the ruleset layer instead of
        documenting it. PHPCS resolves a ruleset-declared severity per message
        code in File::addMessage(), statically, and blind to which route
        supplied the gate value, so this one declaration covers all three of
        them. The suppression is deliberately total: no command-line severity
        flag can undo it, because a warning-severity of 0 hides every warning
        outright and any higher value still loses the `configSeverity` greater
        than `severity` comparison against 0.

        This is why the declaration lives here rather than in rules.xml.
        CleanCode/ruleset.xml is independently loadable, so a consumer may
        point a standard argument straight at it, and a backstop that lived
        only in rules.xml would leave that path exposed. rules.xml pulls this
        file in by reference and must not re-declare a nonzero severity for the
        code afterwards, or the hazard reopens on the very file consumers
        install.

        The escape hatch is a consumer's own ruleset, which is the one place
        that can legitimately want the numbers back: reference the message code
        after the rule ref to rules.xml and give it a nonzero severity. PHPCS
        applies rules in document order, so the later declaration wins.
        tests/Standards/NumberOfChildrenTest.php does exactly that, and is the
        worked example.
    -->
    <rule ref="CleanCode.Metrics.NumberOfChildren.OrdinalIndex">
        <severity>0</severity>
    </rule>
</ruleset>
